steps.instructions:
Research only the angle in your item, from evidence: the code (file:line), its dependencies and docs, and measurements you can take without changing anything (timings, counts, sizes, versions, a dry run). Edit nothing. Use the web research below for anything outside the repository, and cite its URLs; when your angle needs more from outside, read the web yourself (WebSearch and WebFetch when you have them, else a read-only `curl -sL <url>`) and name each URL you used. Give numbers wherever you can and say how you got each one. Name the risks and what the angle would cost to build. End with VERDICT: promising | possible | no.
What the web research found:
{{data.web_docs_text}}

Write the spike's findings for the person who decides: the question; per angle what was found, with its data and evidence; a comparison table (angle, verdict, the key numbers, risk, effort); what is confirmed and what is not (a fact is confirmed only when a source says it: give its URL or file:line; anything else is unconfirmed, with what a human should supply to settle it); a list of the sources with their URLs; open questions; and one recommendation with its reason. RECOMMEND is that recommendation on one line.
The web research (sources and confidence):
{{data.web_docs_text}}
